          NEW ALPHA MICROPROCESSORS, DIGITAL FX!32 SOFTWARE
       LEAD WINDOWS PERFORMANCE BLITZ AT WinHEC '96 BOOTH 438


SAN JOSE, Calif., April 1, 1996 -- Superior Alpha performance, x86 
application compatibility, and multimedia for Windows are highlights 
of the exhibit by Digital Semiconductor, a Digital Equipment 
Corporation business, at WinHEC '96, the annual Windows Hardware 
Engineering Conference open today through Thursday at the San Jose
Convention Center.

     At Booth 438, Digital Semiconductor is demonstrating 
workstations powered by its latest Alpha 21164 microprocessor, 
running at 400MHz, in performance comparisons with systems using 
Intel's newest Pentium and Pentium Pro chips. A "test drive" of the 
Digital FX!32 translation software for 32-bit Windows applications 
will be a major highlight of the exhibit, featuring side-by-side
Alpha and Pentium systems running popular Windows office 
productivity, imaging, and games applications.
    
Performance Without Penalty

     "Visitors to our booth will discover that with Alpha and 
Digital FX!32 products, they can have both unprecedented performance 
and the entire x86 32-bit application suite -- without penalties," 
said Tim Miller, Digital Semiconductor's strategic marketing manager 
for Alpha microprocessors.

     "Alpha performance enables greater creativity and innovation 
for engineers and artists. Alpha really is the Windows processor of 
choice for high-compute applications such as image rendering, 3-D 
animation, and electronic and mechanical computer-aided design," 
Miller added.
     
Popular Applications, Video Conferencing

     The Alpha performance demonstration compares the performance of 
an Alpha 21164-366MHz microprocessor-based system to that of a 
200MHz Pentium Pro system, each running popular computer-aided 
design (CAD) applications including AutoDesk's AutoCAD C4 and 
Bentley Systems' MicroStation software, and 3-D imaging products 
including Elastic Reality and Softimage packages.

     The Digital FX!32 Test Drive enables visitors to sample
popular office, imaging creation and editing, and games applications 
for Windows NT, including Microsoft Office, CORELDRAW, Adobe 
Photoshop, and Microsoft Pinball software. Comparing Alpha and 
Pentium application performance shows how Digital FX!32 technology 
enables Alpha systems to run 32-bit Windows software "out of the 
box" at up to 70 percent of native Alpha speed.

     Booth 438's multimedia display features the first public 
showing of the recently announced 21230 video codec (coder/decoder) 
chip for videoconferencing and multimedia authoring. Two Pentium 
PCs equipped with a 21230-based board exhibit full duplex ISDN 
conferencing. A second area in the booth demonstrates realtime 
MPEG-1 video capture and encode with the 21230 chip, using input 
from a live camera in the booth.

     "With the 21230 chip, videoconferencing is within the reach of 
many Pentium users," said Frank Schapfel, Digital Semiconductor's 
multimedia marketing manager. "The 21230 video codec chip is also 
the only codec on the market that can capture MPEG-1 in both I-frame 
and IBBP-frame formats in real-time. Multimedia developers now have 
greater flexibility in maximizing the video content from their
Windows systems."
     
Alpha PC Board, PCI Peripheral Chips

     The Alpha PC164, an Alpha 21164 board design, appears in both 
functional and static displays, showing its standard PC form factor 
and use of standard, board-level PC components. Digital 
Semiconductor's PCI Ethernet/Fast Ethernet chips, PCI-to-PCI bridge 
chips, and evaluation board products are also on display.

     Digital Semiconductor, a Digital Equipment Corporation business 
headquartered in Hudson, Massachusetts, designs, manufactures and 
markets industry-leading semiconductor products including Alpha 
microprocessors and PCI chips for networking, bridging, and 
multimedia, as well as low-power StrongARM microprocessors under 
license from Advanced RISC Machines Ltd. Mitsubishi Electric 
Company is a second source for Alpha microprocessors. World Wide 
Web site: http://www.digital.com/info/semiconductor/

