            DIGITAL ANNOUNCES VERSION 3.2 OF DECMESSAGEQ


        New MVS Client & Bridging Software To IBM's MQSeries


MAYNARD, Mass., April 15, 1996 --- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced Version 3.2 of its DECmessageQ software product, 
which provides major enhancements to Digital's industry-leading 
message queuing software, including the ability to easily exchange 
information with systems supported by IBM's MQSeries and MVS 
systems.

New features include:

     * 	The DECmessageQ MVS Client - which allows DECmessageQ
        customers to extend the high performance and scalability of 
        DECmessageQ into their MVS mainframe with TCP/IP. This 
        allows a single message queuing API for all application 
        environments.

     * 	The DECmessageQ MQSeries Connection - which provides 
        interoperability between DECmessageQ platforms and any 
        MQSeries platforms through bridging software running on 
        either HP-UX or IBM AIX operating systems.  Customers are 
        now able to mix and match their message queuing middleware 
        to take advantage of the product that best fits their 
        application requirements and budget.

     * 	Improved performance and scalability - DECmessageQ (V3.2) 
        provides more than 40 percent higher message throughput on 
        multi-processor UNIX servers. A single AlphaServer running 
        Digital UNIX or OpenVMS has been benchmarked supporting more 
        than 1,000 simultaneous connections from PC or UNIX 
        desktops.

     * 	Expanded support to 32-bit libraries for Windows 95, 
     	Windows NT, and OS/2. Upgraded support for Visual Basic 
        (Version 4.0) with a 32-bit Dynamic Link Library (DLL) or 
        OLE Custom Control (OCX). 

     DECmessageQ provides enterprise connectivity by integrating 
multiple thousands of PC and UNIX desktops with a wide choice of 
departmental servers and mainframe servers such as IBM's MVS and 
64-bit Digital UNIX. DECmessageQ brings recoverable message 
communication to Digital's industry-leading clustering technology, 
giving users the ultimate in high-availability systems.

     Adding to the connectivity capability of DECmessageQ (V3.2) are 
client/server products, such as the DECmessageQ R/3 Business Bus 
which provides distributed access to SAP R/3 systems. The 
DECmessageQ R/3 Business Bus integration toolkit and services are 
available from Digital's Systems Integration group.

     DECmessageQ (V3.2) is available immediately worldwide. 
Development licenses are priced in the United States from $558 for 
the desktop to $12,955 for the enterprise server. Run-time licenses 
are priced from $186 for the desktop to $5,967 for the enterprise 
server.

     The DECmessageQ MVS Client will be available for ordering 
worldwide beginning in mid-June 1996, and is priced at $19,995 per 
MVS mainframe with unlimited system use. The DECmessageQ MQSeries 
Connection will be available for ordering worldwide beginning May 
30, 1996, and is priced at $4,029 for the departmental server and 
$8,424 for the enterprise server.

     In a performance evaluation of Message Oriented Middleware 
(MOM) products recently completed by Information Design, 
Incorporated (IDI) of Madison, Connecticut, an independent 
middleware research, testing, and consulting firm, Digital's 
DECmessageQ clearly emerges as the performance leader.

     "We were significantly impressed by the findings throughout the 
testing," noted IDI President Ian Kinkade. 

     "In one group of tests run on HP9000/I70 with 2,000 byte 
messages, DECmessageQ delivered 2,004 messages per second while 
IBM's MQSeries only delivered 91 messages per second.

     "This was so dramatic that we ran these tests multiple times to 
ensure their accuracy, and our data was confirmed every time," 
Kinkade added.

     "Today's announcement is in direct response to the rapidly 
growing demand from application developers and systems integrators 
for a better way to extend message queuing multivendor coverage, 
enhance interoperability with IBM environments, and provide improved 
performance and simplified configuration for UNIX systems," said 
Jesse Lipcon, Digital's vice president of Systems Product Management 
and Development. 

     "DECmessageQ (V3.2) now connects Digital's AlphaServers 
and Microsoft's Windows 95 and Windows NT with IBM's MVS mainframes 
and IBM's MQSeries to offer a message queuing backbone for any 
enterprise," Lipcon added. 

     "Digital's enhanced DECmessageQ (V3.2) is more scalable, 
faster, easier to use, less costly, and, as the IDI study shows, 
significantly outperforms the competition," Lipcon said. 

CUSTOMER COMMENT

     Science Applications International Corporation (SAIC) of 
Anchorage, Alaska - a solution provider which develops software
applications for both government and private sector customers - is 
among many companies using Digital's message queuing technology. 
They are currently developing major enhancements to an automated 
command and control application called the Battlefield Situation 
Display (BSD).

     Ron Russell, senior software engineer at SAIC noted recently 
that "military training and combat conditions occasionally 
experience communication failures and outages, both temporary and 
permanent. With DECmessageQ queuing and message recovery features, 
data can be delivered automatically once communications are re-
established. DECmessageQ is critical to the success of this project 
as it supports our design requirements for efficient and reliable 
message-based data transport, storage, and recovery."

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems. Digital's Intel and Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
