           DIGITAL ANNOUNCES AVAILABILITY OF NEW FAMILY OF
               ALPHA-BASED VME SINGLE BOARD COMPUTERS


    ...Engineered To Meet The Increasingly Demanding Performance
          Requirements of Embedded and Realtime Markets...


MAYNARD, Mass., April 24, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced the availability of a new family of Digital Alpha 
VME single board computers (SBCs), based on the 21064A Alpha 
microprocessor. This new family of boards, the Digital Alpha VME 
4/224 and 4/288, extends the capabilities of the Alpha VME family to 
a new level of performance -- offering double the CPU performance of 
Digital's existing AXPvme 230 board.

     The new SBCs -- utilizing the peripheral component interconnect 
(PCI) standard as the on-board bus for the interconnection of high 
performance SCSI, Ethernet and VME interfaces -- are perfectly 
suited for high performance environments such as industrial 
automation, military defense and communications.  The 21064A 
microprocessor provides memory bandwidth four times greater than 
earlier solutions based on the 21066 processor and can be configured 
with up to 2 MB of cache memory.  This new family of boards can be 
configured to accept two single width or one double width PCI 
mezzanine cards (PMCs) with the addition of an optional PMC I/O 
companion card.

     The Digital Alpha VME 4/224 and 4/288 boards are available in 
two CPU speed variants, 224 MHz and 288 MHz, and have been 
engineered to meet the increasingly demanding requirements of the 
embedded and realtime markets.  The Alpha VME 4/288 SBC has been 
estimated to deliver over 200 SPECint92 and over 250 SPECfp92.  With 
this product family, OEM customers gain the reliability and 
performance found in the AlphaServer and AlphaStation systems in the 
form factor of a 6Ux160mm VME module.  In addition, the CPU design 
of these new systems utilizes the same core logic found in Digital's 
AlphaServer 1000 system.     

Robust Software Environment Supports New Product Family

     Understanding the mission critical importance of software in 
providing a complete solution, Digital offers two state-of-the art 
realtime operating systems to support this new family of boards and 
provide solutions to the wide range of performance and functionality 
issues related to high-performance embedded systems.  Digital UNIX 
and VxWorks for Alpha support key industry standards which enable 
application interoperability and portability between the two 
environments.

     The VxWorks for Alpha microkernel is specifically designed for 
embedded applications requiring high performance and deterministic 
behavior.  It supports industry standards including UNIX, realtime 
POSIX 1003.1, TCP/IP, Ethernet, X Window System, C and Ada.  VxWorks 
features a priority-based fully preemptive multitasking kernel.  In 
addition, VxWorks for Alpha does not require disk, but offers SCSI 
disk support and MS-DOS, RT-11 and raw file systems for applications 
that require disk I/O.

     Digital UNIX is the industry's only 64-bit UNIX system and 
supports the broadest set of industry UNIX standards, including 
XPG4, 1170, SVID, POSIX, AES and MOTIF.  Existing UNIX applications 
can easily be ported to Alpha.  The fully preemptive kernel and 
priority-based scheduler in Digital UNIX provides outstanding 
realtime performance in a full function, disk-based commercial UNIX 
operating system.

Pricing and Availability

     The Alpha VME 4/224 board prices range from $8,000 to $15,000, 
and the Alpha VME 4/288 board prices range between $11,000 and 
$17,000, depending on configuration.  Both boards will ship in July 
1996.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's Intel and Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
